sudan and ethiopia are facing off in a  border confrontation that could redraw  the map of east africa  on the surface the dispute seems simple  with both sides claiming al-fashaga a  valuable region of farmland as their own  but look deeper and the situation is  more complex  the war in tigre the struggle over the  nile the eritrea factor egypt all of  these things just make for a much more  combustible  mix ethiopia is already embroiled in a  brutal civil war  a new international conflict would prove  devastating not just for ethiopia but  the entire region  al-fasharga  a stolen land from both sides  perspectives a stolen land that has been  retaken by force  [Music]  as with many borders in africa the  problems began with the british  while ethiopia remained independent for  most of the colonial era sudan was  colonized by britain  eager to demarcate its new territory  britain drew the border between ethiopia  and sudan in 1902  and one area was a bone of contention  from the beginning alpha saga  alfa saga area which is around 250  kilometers is extremely fertile and  europa farmers used to grow sesame seeds  one of the main export items for addis  ababa  even though the region was inhabited by  both ethiopian and sudanese farmers the  british claimed the area as part of  sudan  a decision that many ethiopians never  accepted  the way the ethio sudan border has been  demarcated  is problematic from the get-go it cuts  across same communities  that shares same values culture custom  language tradition and sometimes even  religion  over the decades tensions on the border  have regularly erupted into violence  but in 2008 a landmark treaty was agreed  al-fasaga was recognized by both sides  as sudanese  but in return ethiopian farmers would be  able to live and work freely in the  region both sides were pacified for a  while  but peace didn't hold for long  in 2018  ethiopia appointed a new prime minister  from the central region of the country  abi ahmed  he was seen as a reformist someone who  was liberalizing the country's politics  and its economy it brought his immense  hope and optimism both to the country  and and the region at large i mean we  talked at the time about abby mania he  was that popular and in 2019 he won a  nobel peace prize for his part in  seemingly settling a border disagreement  which had lasted for two decades between  ethiopia and eritrea  you  represent  a new generation of african leaders  despite his popularity abroad abby's  rise was resented by some at home  especially ethiopia's former ruling  party the tigray people's liberation  front and their supporters based in the  north of the country  keen to cement his leadership abby  wasted no time in criticizing his  predecessors  [Music]  that they were being scapegoated for  some of the the troubles of the previous  three decades authoritarianism  corruption so whilst much the country  welcomed the new prime minister  in tigre there was  apprehension and concern  which built up over the subsequent years  the situation came to a head in 2020  abby's government delayed a general  election citing the pandemic as the  cause  officials in tigre already mistrustful  of the government were furious and  decided to go ahead with their election  anyway  it was a direct challenge to abby's  leadership  well both sides started engaging in kind  of mutual delegitimization accusing the  other of being in power illegally in  november 2020. to graham forces attacked  federal military bases abby launched an  all-out assault on the region the  federal government  had every right  to deploy federal security forces  in an attempt to hamper rebel supply  lines into tigray abbey asked sudan to  seal the border between ethiopia and the  contested region of alpha shaga  sudan sensed an opportunity to claim  alpha saga once and for all  this is you know ethiopia at this point  was  was distracted it was plunging into a  civil conflict and it was weak so the  sudanese army went further than simply  securing the border they evicted  ethiopian farmers in the area they  heavily militarized the area  the farmers evicted by sudan were  amharas an ethnic group whose support  was vital to abi in his fight against  segre  now under a huge amount of pressure  abby's response was resolute  [Music]  ethiopia for the first time in in some  time claimed that this was in fact  ethiopian territory that this was an  invasion in this period of of of calm  has essentially been torn up now i mean  we're back to a point where both sides  lay claim to the territory you have  multiple different  armed forces  including ethnic militia in the area  which makes for an incredibly volatile  mix  after months of skirmishes sudan and  ethiopia are now at a standoff  it's a potentially explosive situation  especially as both countries are already  jostling for control of the region's  most precious resource water  the grand ethiopian renaissance dam has  also been contentious from the start  situated in ethiopia on the blue nile  just 20 kilometers from the sudanese  border the dam will transform ethiopia's  economy enabling it to export cheap  electricity to the whole region but for  downstream neighbors sudan and egypt who  rely on the blue nile ethiopia's control  of their water supply is deeply  unsettling  if the water that's coming to egypt  reduced by two percent at about one  million will be jobless  egypt who for whom denial is everything  are the waters of the nile it's  absolutely it's life lifeblood and  anything that potentially hinders it is  seen as a potential death knell for the  for the country  with concerns over the dam growing sudan  is increasingly siding with egypt  in 2021 the country's joined in a  military operation called guardians of  the nile  i think it's quite clear that the  sudanese have started to  to see the problems of the dam  through an egyptian lens to an extent  and they certainly started to express  their concerns regarding the safety of  their own damn downstream  so far efforts to bring sudan and  ethiopia to the table have been  unsuccessful  but some hope that the african union a  pan-african organization created to  promote peace could play a role in  encouraging both sides to step back from  the brink  we need to see a more meaningful  structured  mediation process that comes from the  africa union i think that will also pave  the way for similar contested borders to  be resolved in an unemployment  sustainable way so the international  community needs to support the african  union but i think  the initiative  the mediation process  the ownership should come from the  region itself  thanks for watching if you'd like to  read more of the economist east africa  coverage click the link and don't forget  to subscribe  you
 

